**user**:
> I want to impute R2 values on regression models, based on a dataset with missing R2 value data. What are appropriate references to best practices in the literature on imputing summary statistics such as R²?
**assistant**:
> There isn’t much literature specifically on *imputing missing \(R^2\) values* as a summary statistic. The best practice references are mostly indirect, coming from:
> 
> 1. **general missing-data / multiple imputation theory**
> 2. **meta-analysis with missing or incompletely reported effect sizes**
> 3. **guidance on passive imputation / deterministic transformations**
> 4. **cautions about imputing outcomes derived from other quantities**
> 
> ## Short answer
> 
> If you’re imputing missing \(R^2\) values from other study/model characteristics, the strongest literature base is:
> 
> - **Rubin (1987)** for the core logic of multiple imputation
> - **van Buuren (2018)** for practical best practices
> - **Azur et al. (2011)** for accessible MI guidance
> - **White, Royston, & Wood (2011)** for MICE guidance and pitfalls
> - **Enders (2010)** for applied missing-data methods
> - **Pigott (2001, 2009)** for missing data in meta-analysis
> - **Debray et al.** and related prediction-model meta-analysis papers if your regressions are predictive models
> - **Harel (2009)** and related work for pooling \(R^2\) across imputations, if your issue is \(R^2\) after MI rather than imputing reported \(R^2\) itself
> 
> ## Key conceptual point
> 
> \(R^2\) is a **bounded, derived statistic**:
> - bounded in \([0,1]\) for ordinary \(R^2\)
> - can be negative for some out-of-sample or pseudo-\(R^2\) contexts
> - depends on sample size, number of predictors, outcome variance, model specification, and sometimes estimation method
> 
> So the literature would generally say:
> 
> - **prefer imputing the underlying variables** from which \(R^2\) could be computed, if available
> - if only study-level summaries are available, use a **substantive model** for the summary statistic and respect its bounds/distribution
> - do **multiple imputation**, not single imputation
> - conduct **sensitivity analysis**
> - be explicit that imputing a reported performance statistic is potentially high-risk unless strong auxiliary variables exist
> 
> ## Best references
> 
> ### Core missing-data / MI references
> 
> **Rubin, D. B. (1987).** *Multiple Imputation for Nonresponse in Surveys.* Wiley.  
> Classic foundational reference. Best for justifying MI instead of ad hoc single imputation.
> 
> **van Buuren, S. (2018).** *Flexible Imputation of Missing Data* (2nd ed.). CRC Press.  
> Probably the most useful practical reference. Relevant topics:
> - imputation of bounded variables
> - transformations
> - passive imputation for derived quantities
> - predictive mean matching vs parametric models
> - diagnostics and congeniality
> 
> **Enders, C. K. (2010).** *Applied Missing Data Analysis.* Guilford Press.  
> Strong applied statistical treatment, including when imputation models are plausible and when they are fragile.
> 
> **Azur, M. J., Stuart, E. A., Frangakis, C., & Leaf, P. J. (2011).**  
> Multiple imputation by chained equations: What is it and how does it work? *International Journal of Methods in Psychiatric Research, 20*(1), 40–49.  
> Good lightweight citation for MICE.
> 
> **White, I. R., Royston, P., & Wood, A. M. (2011).**  
> Multiple imputation using chained equations: Issues and guidance for practice. *Statistics in Medicine, 30*(4), 377–399.  
> A very good best-practices paper. Especially useful for:
> - selecting predictors for the imputation model
> - including the analysis model in the imputation model
> - transformations and non-normal variables
> - avoiding uncongenial or incompatible imputations
> 
> ## References on derived / transformed quantities
> 
> For \(R^2\), a useful framing is that it is a transformed proportion-like quantity. Best practice often suggests imputing on a transformed scale if imputing the summary itself.
> 
> **van Buuren (2018)** again is the main practical source here.
> 
> You may also want to cite:
> **Little, R. J. A., & Rubin, D. B. (2019).** *Statistical Analysis with Missing Data* (3rd ed.). Wiley.  
> General theory; supports the principle that imputing derived summaries directly is less ideal than imputing source data when possible.
> 
> ## Meta-analysis / missing summary-statistic literature
> 
> If your setting is a literature review or evidence synthesis where papers omit \(R^2\), these are more relevant.
> 
> **Pigott, T. D. (2001).**  
> A review of methods for missing data in educational research. *Educational Research and Evaluation, 7*(4), 353–383.
> 
> **Pigott, T. D. (2009).**  
> Handling missing data in research synthesis. In *The Handbook of Research Synthesis and Meta-Analysis* (2nd ed.).  
> Useful for the principle that missing study-level summaries should be handled with model-based approaches and sensitivity analysis.
> 
> **Pigott, T. D. (2012).** *Advances in Meta-Analysis.* Springer.  
> Includes discussion of missing effect-size information and related synthesis issues.
> 
> If you are using \(R^2\) in a meta-regression or study-comparison context, these are defensible references, though again they will not be specifically about \(R^2\).
> 
> ## If your regressions are prediction models
> 
> There is a literature on meta-analysis of prediction model performance statistics where \(R^2\) can appear alongside C-statistics, calibration slope, etc.
> 
> Useful references include:
> 
> **Debray, T. P. A., et al. (2017).**  
> A guide to systematic review and meta-analysis of prediction model performance. *BMJ, 356*, i6460.  
> This is more about synthesizing reported model performance than imputing it, but it gives a principled framework for handling incomplete reporting.
> 
> **Riley, R. D., Debray, T. P. A., et al.** papers on meta-analysis of prediction model performance.  
> These emphasize incomplete reporting and the importance of recovering or reconstructing performance statistics when possible rather than naively imputing them.
> 
> ## If your problem is actually “pooling \(R^2\) after multiple imputation”
> 
> This is a different question, but often confused with yours.
> 
> **Harel, O. (2009).**  
> The estimation of \(R^2\) and adjusted \(R^2\) in incomplete data sets using multiple imputation. *Journal of Applied Statistics, 36*(10), 1109–1118.  
> This is one of the most directly relevant papers if you are fitting regressions to multiply imputed data and want pooled \(R^2\).
> 
> Also relevant:
> **Harel, O. (2012)** and related MI pooling papers on transformed or bounded estimands.  
> These are more about combining estimates after MI than imputing missing published \(R^2\).
> 
> ## Practical best-practice guidance you can defend
> 
> If you need language for a methods section, something like this would be supportable from the literature:
> 
> 1. **Do not treat missing \(R^2\) as missing completely at random unless justified.**  
>    Missing reporting of model fit is often informative.
> 
> 2. **Prefer reconstruction over imputation.**  
>    If you have enough information to derive \(R^2\) approximately from other statistics, that is often preferable to free-form imputation.
> 
> 3. **If imputing \(R^2\) directly, use multiple imputation with auxiliary variables** such as:
>    - sample size
>    - number of predictors
>    - adjusted vs unadjusted \(R^2\)
>    - model family
>    - outcome domain
>    - in/out-of-sample status
>    - other fit statistics (AIC, RMSE, correlation, F-statistic, etc.)
> 
> 4. **Respect the bounded scale.**  
>    Impute on a transformed scale, e.g.:
>    - logit(\(R^2\)) for values strictly in \((0,1)\)
>    - or use beta-regression-based imputation if appropriate
>    - with care for exact 0 or 1 values
> 
> 5. **Use sensitivity analyses.**  
>    Compare:
>    - complete-case analysis
>    - MI under MAR
>    - alternative imputation specifications
>    - possibly MNAR sensitivity scenarios if nonreporting may depend on the true \(R^2\)
> 
> 6. **Report uncertainty from imputation properly** using Rubin’s rules or appropriate pooling methods.
> 
> ## What I would cite first
> 
> If I had to choose a concise, defensible reference set, I’d use:
> 
> - **Rubin (1987)**
> - **White, Royston, & Wood (2011)**
> - **van Buuren (2018)**
> - **Pigott (2009 or 2012)** if this is study-summary/meta-analytic
> - **Harel (2009)** if the context is regression \(R^2\) with MI datasets
> 
> ## Important caution
> 
> If what you really have is a dataset of models and some are missing reported \(R^2\), imputing those \(R^2\) values may be statistically weak unless you have strong predictors of model performance. In many cases, reviewers will prefer:
> - recovering the original model outputs,
> - reconstructing \(R^2\) from reported test statistics,
> - or analyzing with missingness explicitly rather than filling in a performance metric.
> 
> That is, the literature supports MI in general, but it does **not** strongly endorse casual imputation of a goodness-of-fit summary without a strong substantive model.
> 
